From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DjDeveloperr <hey@djdev.me>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 07:02:24 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] soc: apple: sep: let keybag creation rewrite its shared xART
 record

On a MacBook Pro M2 Pro (T6020, J414s), a second Linux install never got
a Touch ID keybag. CREATE_KEYBAG makes the enclave rewrite one existing
kind-4 xART record. That record is one of the four kind-4 records the
enclave reads at every attach. A 2026-09-26 capture from the first Linux
install already has it, and macOS's enclave rewrote it on 2026-11-02
while no Linux install was running. It predates every Linux install, so
it is never on Linux's list, and xart_guard=1 refused it. The enclave
did not fail the create. It stopped answering, and the hardware RNG and
every later key-store request timed out. The driver also kept its create
intent, so every later boot refused to create a keybag ("identity keybag
state is ambiguous: EEXIST"), and trusted keys failed with EEXIST.

The other Linux install is not the cause. Every guarded keybag creation
on that Mac asks for the same rewrite. The first install has a keybag
only because it was made before the guard existed. An M1 Pro (T6000)
rewrites one pre-Linux attach record the same way. It runs the same
stub sepOS, so the risk to macOS is the same, and so far it has needed
xart_guard=0.

Run the identity CREATE_KEYBAG in the window a boot policy change
already uses. It allows one rewrite per boot, of one kind-4 record that
existed at probe. It never allows a delete or another kind, the roots
follow the level, the previous value is saved on the host first, and
nothing is adopted. The exchange now waits up to 6 s, as a 13.5 key
store's does, because the saved value and the new slot are flushed
inside it. Every other session record that existed at probe stays
refused, before, during and after the create. That includes any record
another Linux install made.

Make an unfinished create recoverable. The intent now records how long
the guard's list was. New session records are listed before they are
written, so a later boot whose list has not grown knows the create left
nothing behind, and sends it again. An intent from an earlier kernel
records no length. It is sent again only while the list is empty, which
recovers the installs this refusal already left stuck.

A guarded cold-boot Mac is no longer reported as matching "blocked"
just for being cold-boot. That rule assumed its keybag could only be
created with the guard off. It is now "unverified", like a warm-attach
Mac, until the guard refuses a write. aurora-sep-ready drops the
matching reason, and the ABI doc and XART-SAFETY.md follow.

KUnit covers the window's terms, the list length kept from load, the
retry rule, and the intent format, including the zero-UUID intent that
earlier kernels wrote.

Signed-off-by: DjDeveloperr <hey@djdev.me>
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
(cherry picked from commit 15825b40d4186c069d313d98e07d99c02174b1b2)

[j604: resolved onto the omacom first-setup window: open_first_setup()/
 close_first_setup() kept around the windowed CREATE_KEYBAG exchange; docs
 merged; aurora-sep PKGBUILD sums recomputed from the linked sources.]

